.TH RXEJIT 1 "Aug 2026" Linux "User Manuals"
.SH NAME
rxejit \- JIT-compile a regular-expression set into native code and run it: enumerate, count, keycrack (CPU or GPU), or find duplicates
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rxejit
[\fB-S\fR]
[\fB-n\fR | \fB-m\fR \fIfile\fR [\fB-H\fR \fImd5\fR|\fIntlm\fR|\fIsha1\fR|\fIsha256\fR] | \fB-d\fR [\fB-v\fR]]
[\fB-G\fR]
[\fB-C\fR]
[\fB-j\fR \fIjobs\fR]
[\fB-p\fR \fIsec\fR]
[\fB-D\fR \fIdir\fR]
.I regex
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B rxejit
takes the (mostly Perl-compatible) regular expression in
.I regex
as the specification of a finite set, and instead of interpreting it,
.B compiles
it: it emits a purpose-built C program (for the GPU, an OpenCL program) whose
core is an unrolled odometer over fixed character buffers, one wheel per varying
position, delta-patched as the odometer turns. That program is then built with
the system C compiler and run. The regex is the machine; the emitted code is a
straight-line enumerator with no interpreter left in the inner loop.
.PP
This makes the walk very fast, which matters because the interesting jobs are
per-member: sift a set against a target list, hash every member and look for a
match (keycracking), or hash every member into a set and report collisions
(deduplication). See
.BR rxenum (1)
for the set language itself and the counting/enumeration semantics;
.B rxejit
shares the parser and the enumeration order but trades startup (a compile step)
for a much faster inner loop.
.PP
.B rxejit
handles any finite pattern \-\- masks, alternations, bounded repeats,
dictionaries, backreferences, ordered and unordered combinations
.RB ( re ){{ ... }},
and policy compositions
.RB ( A | B | ... ){{ n , m ! floors }}.
Only an unbounded (infinite) repeat, or a set too large to unroll within the
limits below, is declined \-\- with a printed reason.
.PP
As regular expressions often contain characters special to the shell, enclose
.I regex
in single quotes.
.SH OPTIONS
With no sink switch,
.B rxejit
enumerates and prints every member (the compiled equivalent of
.BR "rxenum -n" ).
.TP
.B \-n
Count the members rather than print them. Times the walk with no I/O \-\- useful
to measure raw throughput.
.TP
.B \-m \fIfile\fR
Sift: print only the members that appear in
.IR file
(one target per line). The pattern is a keyspace and
.I file
is the set to match it against.
.TP
.B \-H \fIalg\fR
With
.BR -m ,
.I file
holds hex digests of one hash and each candidate is hashed and compared \-\- this
is keycracking. Output is
.IR digest : plaintext
per hit.
.I alg
is one of
.BR md5 ", " ntlm ", " sha1 ", " sha256 .
.B ntlm
is MD4 of the UTF-16LE encoding (the Windows password hash).
.TP
.B \-d
Report duplicate members: hash each into a per-thread set and merge at the join.
Exit status 0 if all members are distinct, 1 if any repeats.
.IP
Note that this is less useful than it looks: the set is held in memory, so it
grows with the member count and blows up long before the sets you would actually
find interesting. In practice it only works on small patterns \-\- using it on a
large one is flirting with the OOM killer.
.TP
.B \-v
With
.BR -d ,
list the repeated members and their counts.
.TP
.B \-G
Run the keycrack on the GPU via OpenCL (see
.B THE GPU BACKEND
below). Requires
.B \-m
.I file
.B \-H
.IR alg .
One OpenCL program is generated per pattern shape and built at run time.
.TP
.B \-j \fIjobs\fR
Number of CPU threads for the
.BR -n ", " -m " and " -d
runs (default: one per CPU). Printing stays single-threaded and ordered.
.TP
.B \-p \fIsec\fR
Every
.I sec
seconds, print progress to stderr: percent done, rate, elapsed and ETA. The
threaded
.BR -n / -m
runs and
.B -G
report it;
.B -G
also reports GPU occupancy (the fraction of wall time the device was busy).
.TP
.B \-C
Force the scalar hash path (no AVX2 md5/md4, no SHA-NI) for this run, for an
A/B against the accelerated path on the same machine. Sets
.BR RXEJIT_NOACCEL ,
which any generated binary (see
.BR -S )
also honours.
.TP
.B \-D \fIdir\fR
Also look in
.I dir
for a
.BI [: name :]
dictionary's
.IB name .dict
file (one word per line). May be given more than once.
.TP
.B \-S
Print the generated C (or, with
.BR -G ,
OpenCL host + kernel) to stdout instead of compiling and running it. The
device kernel source is the
.B KSRC
C string in the output. Invaluable for inspecting exactly what a pattern
compiles to.
.SH KEYCRACKING
The core keycrack workflow is:
.PP
.RS
.nf
rxejit \-m \fIhashes.txt\fR \-H md5 '[a-z]{8}'
.fi
.RE
.PP
which hashes every member of the mask, looks each digest up in the sorted target
set, and prints
.IR digest : plaintext
for every hit. Add
.B \-G
to run it on the GPU, and
.B \-p 1
to watch the rate. A target that is not a member makes the run sweep the whole
keyspace (a throughput measurement); the run stops early once every distinct
target has been cracked.
.SH THE GPU BACKEND
With
.BR -G ,
.B rxejit
splits the work between itself (the host, which owns the enumerator) and a
generated OpenCL program (the device half). For a pattern with a fixed-class
tail, the host stride-seeks the head \-\- alternations, dictionaries, head-side
backreferences, anything
.B rxe
enumerates \-\- and streams each high prefix to the device, which sweeps the low
block for it. For a pattern that is wholly a choice or a policy, one grid covers
the entire index space. The device tiles the sweep and double-buffers launches
so it never idles on the host between tiles. Hits are recorded as plaintext and
re-hashed on the host.
.PP
Which kernel a pattern compiles to is decided at build time from its shape.
Inspect the choice, and the generated code, with
.BR "-S -G" .
.SS The kernels
.TP
.B fixed-mask (generic, crackG)
A run of independent fixed character classes \-\- a plain mask like
.BR [a-z]{8} ,
or the fixed-class tail of a structured pattern. Each lane decodes its index
into the per-position digits and lays the bytes from a merged wheel table; the
member width and hash length are baked in, so the sweep runs at full speed. This
is the common case and the fastest per candidate.
.TP
.B variable-length (lowvar, crackG)
A dictionary or an uneven alternation with no fixed tail worth splitting \-\-
e.g.
.BR [:efflarge:]{6} .
Each lane lays each chosen word's real bytes compactly, advancing a running
length, then hashes that length. One hash block covers any member under the
block size, so only the byte-copy diverges between lanes.
.TP
.B permutation / combination (crackP)
An ordered
.RB ( re ){{ n , m ! }}
or unordered
.RB ( re ){{ n , m }}
choice from a pool. Each lane unranks its index into the chosen items \-\-
factorial number system for a permutation, colex/binomial for a combination \-\-
tracking only the k picks, so nothing pool-wide sits in private memory. The pool
is a
.B __global
buffer, so a large dictionary pool is fine.
.TP
.B policy (crackP)
A policy composition
.RB ( A | B | ... ){{ n , m ! floors }}
\-\- the password-policy operator (at least so many characters of each class).
Each lane decodes its index into a segment (a length + class-count vector,
minimal-compliance first), an arrangement (which class fills each position) and
the character values, then lays the member.
.TP
.B loop-repeat (hybrid, crackL)
A bare single-wheel bounded repeat like
.BR [a-z]{1,8} ,
swept split by length. Surrounding or multi-wheel structure is not handled by
this kernel and stays on the CPU.
.SS The incremental odometer (RXEJIT_GPU_RUN)
By default each lane cracks one candidate: it decodes that candidate's whole
odometer from its global index every time. The
.B incremental odometer
instead gives each lane a contiguous run of
.B RUN
candidates: it decodes the run's start once, then steps the odometer by +1 with
carry, re-laying only what changed. It amortises the per-candidate
.B decode
over the run. Whether that is a win depends entirely on how much the decode
cost \-\- as opposed to the hash \-\- bottlenecks the kernel, and on whether the
+1 step stays coalesced and branch-light.
.PP
The environment variable
.B RXEJIT_GPU_RUN
controls it:
.B RXEJIT_GPU_RUN=1
forces the per-candidate decode (the baseline), for diagnostics, correctness
A/B, or a device where incremental does not help;
.BI RXEJIT_GPU_RUN= N
for
.IR N " > 1"
sets the run length. Every
.B -G
run prints which mode is active, e.g.
.PP
.RS
.nf
rxejit -G: odometer incremental (RUN=16)
.fi
.RE
.PP
The default per kernel, and the measured effect:
.TP
.B fixed-mask \-\- incremental by default (RUN=8)
The decode is trivial (a chain of divides), so there is little to amortise. A
small win on a weak integrated GPU (~1.3x), roughly neutral on a big discrete
GPU. Reverting costs nothing there.
.TP
.B policy \-\- incremental by default (RUN=16)
The decode is expensive (a segment search, a multiset-arrangement unrank, a
character unrank), so amortising it is a clear win: ~1.9x on an RTX 4080 and
~3x on an integrated GPU, converging the kernel on the fixed-mask ceiling. This
is the biggest gain.
.TP
.B variable-length \-\- incremental by default (RUN=16)
A stepped word can change length and shift the tail, so a step re-lays the
suffix from the changed wheel rather than one byte; still cheaper than a full
decode-and-lay. A modest win (~1.25x on an integrated GPU diceware dictionary).
.TP
.B permutation / combination \-\- per-candidate by default
Grid-stride wins here and incremental is
.B not
the default. The unrank is already cheap (a few cached table reads, not the
policy's heavy decode), so there is little to save; and the data-dependent
next-combination step diverges while the contiguous run loses the grid-stride's
coalesced adjacent-lane/adjacent-index sweep. Measured, it
.B regresses
(~0.6x). It can still be selected with
.B RXEJIT_GPU_RUN
for experiments, but expect it to be slower.
.TP
.B loop-repeat \-\- per-candidate (no incremental path)
.PP
Correctness is independent of
.BR RUN :
every kernel returns the identical hit set at any run length, verified against
the per-candidate baseline. The knob is a performance choice only.
.SH THE CPU BACKEND
Without
.BR -G ,
the hash sinks use tuned CPU paths: an 8-way AVX2 core for MD5 and MD4/NTLM, and
SHA-NI instructions for SHA-1 and SHA-256, where the hardware has them, with a
first-word probe filter. A run distributes over
.B -j
threads.
.B -C
(or
.BR RXEJIT_NOACCEL )
forces the scalar path for an A/B on the same machine. AVX2 all-core runs at a
lower clock than scalar, so throughput can scale below the core count while the
part stays cool and power-limited; SHA-NI scales best.
.SH LIMITATIONS AND CAVEATS
.TP
.B One hash block per candidate
A candidate must fit a single hash block (under 56 bytes for MD5/SHA-1/SHA-256;
NTLM's UTF-16LE encoding halves that budget). Wider patterns are declined for
.BR -G .
.TP
.B 64-bit odometer
The member count must fit 64 bits; a larger set is declined ("more members than
fit 64 bits"). This also bounds
.BR -p 's
percent/ETA display.
.TP
.B Recorded-hit cap
.B -G
records at most 2^20 (1,048,576) hits; beyond that it reports the true hit
count but only the first 1,048,576 plaintexts, and \-\- because lanes fill the
buffer in a nondeterministic order \-\- which ones are kept can differ run to
run. This only affects sets where more than a million members are targets (for
example, hashing a whole keyspace back against itself); a normal crack of a real
target list is far under the cap. A full-set A/B between two runs must therefore
use a keyspace with fewer than 1,048,576 members to be byte-exact.
.TP
.B GPU policy bounds
A policy is declined for
.B -G
if it needs more than 32 classes, is wider than 40 positions, or its segment
table would exceed 48 KB.
.TP
.B Unordered-choice table
An unordered choice is declined for
.B -G
if its Pascal (binomial) table would exceed 2^20 entries.
.TP
.B NVIDIA constant memory
NVIDIA's OpenCL caps the number of program-scope
.B __constant
arrays (about 7 including the runtime's own) and the constant bank at 64 KB.
.B rxejit
merges all wheel byte tables into one array to stay under the count, and spills
a large dictionary's tables to
.B __global
past ~48 KB; small tables stay cached in
.BR __constant .
.TP
.B Early exit
.B -G
stops once every distinct target has been cracked (checked at each tile drain).
A target that is not a member, or duplicate target lines, never trips it, so the
run sweeps fully \-\- which is what you want for a throughput measurement.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B RXEJIT_GPU_RUN
Incremental-odometer run length for
.BR -G .
1 = per-candidate (baseline);
.I N
> 1 = incremental with run length
.IR N .
Overrides the per-kernel default. See
.B THE GPU BACKEND
above.
.TP
.B RXEJIT_NOACCEL
Force the scalar hash path (equivalent to
.BR -C ).
Honoured by
.B rxejit
and by any binary it generated.
.TP
.B RXEJIT_NO_UNROLL
Build the OpenCL kernel with
.B -D RXEJIT_UNROLL=0
(disable manual loop unrolling), for an A/B on the device compiler.
.TP
.B CC
The C compiler used to build the generated program (default
.BR cc ).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B 0
on success (for
.BR -d ,
also "no duplicates").
.B 1
from
.B -d
when a duplicate exists.
.B 2
on a usage, compile, or run error (including a pattern declined for the chosen
backend, with a printed reason).
.SH EXAMPLES
Crack a list of MD5 digests over an 8-lowercase-letter keyspace on the GPU,
watching the rate:
.PP
.RS
.nf
rxejit \-G \-p 1 \-m hashes.txt \-H md5 '[a-z]{8}'
.fi
.RE
.PP
Crack a six-word EFF diceware passphrase, dictionaries in ./dicts:
.PP
.RS
.nf
rxejit \-G \-D dicts \-m hashes.txt \-H sha256 '[:efflarge:]{6}'
.fi
.RE
.PP
Crack an eight-character password-policy keyspace (at least two of each class):
.PP
.RS
.nf
rxejit \-G \-m hashes.txt \-H ntlm '([a-z]|[A-Z]|[0-9]){{8!2,2,2}}'
.fi
.RE
.PP
Inspect the generated OpenCL for a pattern without running it:
.PP
.RS
.nf
rxejit \-S \-G \-H md5 \-m /dev/null '[a-z]{8}'
.fi
.RE
.PP
A/B the incremental odometer against the per-candidate baseline for a policy
crack on the same GPU:
.PP
.RS
.nf
RXEJIT_GPU_RUN=1  rxejit \-G \-p 1 \-m hashes.txt \-H md5 '([a-z]|[0-9]){{8!3,3}}'
.br
RXEJIT_GPU_RUN=16 rxejit \-G \-p 1 \-m hashes.txt \-H md5 '([a-z]|[0-9]){{8!3,3}}'
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR rxenum (1),
.BR rxedot (1),
.BR rxerank (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Marco Carnut and the rxe project.
